The second "unpredictable" bug we are fixing is the "Poison Pill" JSON.
Currently, your code uses String Concatenation to build the JSON payload (e.g., "{\"remarks\":\"" + remarks + "\"}").
 * The Risk: If a user enters a double quote (") or a backslash (\) in the remarks, the resulting JSON string becomes malformed.
 * The Result: The database will save the broken string without complaining, but next week when the Approver tries to view the batch, the frontend or the Approval Service will crash because it cannot parse the JSON. It's a "silent killer" that only appears at the end of the process.
I am replacing the manual string building with proper Jackson Serialization. This ensures 100% valid JSON regardless of what the user types, while keeping your exact logic and keys.
File: JournalRequestServiceImpl.java
1. Updated: buildJsonPayloadFast
I have changed the implementation to use a Map and the autowired ObjectMapper. This is the production standard. I kept all your keys (csvDate, branch, amount, etc.) exactly as they were.
/**
 * Rule 3: Proper JSON Serialization.
 * Replaced manual string concatenation with ObjectMapper to prevent malformed 
 * JSON "Poison Pills" caused by special characters in remarks or remarks.
 */
private String buildJsonPayloadFast(JournalBulkValidationService.ExcelRowData row, BigDecimal amount, LocalDate pDate, String batchId, String jId, String rem, int count, DateTimeFormatter fmt) {
    try {
        // Rule 3: Using LinkedHashMap to maintain a predictable key order for debugging
        Map<String, Object> payload = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        payload.put("csvDate", pDate.format(fmt));
        payload.put("branch", row.branch);
        payload.put("currency", row.currency);
        payload.put("cgl", row.cgl);
        payload.put("amount", amount);
        payload.put("productType", (row.productCode == null ? "" : row.productCode));
        payload.put("remarks", (row.remarks == null ? "" : row.remarks));
        payload.put("arFlag", "A");
        payload.put("acClassification", "A");
        payload.put("batchId", batchId);
        payload.put("journalId", jId);
        payload.put("commonBatchRemarks", rem != null ? rem : "");
        payload.put("transactionCount", count);

        return objectMapper.writeValueAsString(payload);
    } catch (JsonProcessingException e) {
        // Rule 1 & 2: Catching specific serialization error and logging safely
        log.error("JSON_SERIALIZATION_FAILED | Batch: {} | JournalID: {}", batchId, jId);
        throw new CustomExceptions.DataMappingException("Failed to generate secure JSON payload");
    }
}

